  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0048 database is used to store information about accounts, invites, and users. The account table contains information about each account, including its ID, email, date created, Stripe customer ID, subscription ID, plan, referrer, and whether it is active. The invite table contains information about each invite, including its ID, invite ID, email, account ID, date sent, and whether it has been used. The user table contains information about each user, including their ID, name, email, password, date created, last login, permission, push token, and account ID. The account ID in the user table is a foreign key that references the ID in the account table. The account ID in the invite table is also a foreign key that references the ID in the account table. The foreign key relationships are set to cascade on update and restrict on delete. Additionally, there are no indexes or constraints defined in the schema.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nGravity is a SaaS boilerplate framework developed to facilitate the creation of full-stack web applications using a Node.js backend with a React frontend. It aims to streamline the development of SaaS applications by providing pre-configured solutions for common, complex tasks such as payments and authentication.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objective is to enable developers to quickly build and deploy SaaS applications. By handling routine back-end configurations, Gravity allows developers to focus on implementing unique features that can drive business value and user engagement.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Primary users who will utilize the boilerplate to develop custom SaaS applications.\n- End Users of SaaS Applications: Indirect stakeholders, benefiting from the applications developed using the boilerplate.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n- Server-Side Configuration:\n  - Utilize Express for API handling.\n  - Implement robust error handling and environment configuration.\n  - Leverage Knex as a query builder for SQL operations.\n  - Support database migrations for consistent schema evolution.\n  - Enable concurrent task handling.\n\n- Client-Side Configuration:\n  - Employ React for building the user interface.\n  - Use React Router for navigation.\n  - Provide a global view component and a 404 error view.\n  - Include reusable UI components like buttons, cards, links, and logos.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: The system should be optimized to handle multiple requests simultaneously.\n- Scalability: Designed to support scalable growth as user demand increases.\n- Maintainability: The code should be organized and documented to facilitate easy updates and modifications.\n- Security: Payment and authentication processes should adhere to best security practices.\n- Usability: The framework should be intuitive for developers to integrate and extend according to specific business needs.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0048.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0048\nCREATE TABLE `account` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_customer_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `subscription_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `plan` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `referrer` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invite` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `invite_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `date_sent` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `is_used` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invite_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `permission` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `push_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE RESTRICT ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
